        "bodyTr": "The government has decided to expand the operational area of the Navy's Cheonghae Unit from the existing Gulf of Aden to waters south of Oman, approximately 650 kilometers in straight-line distance from the Strait of Hormuz. It will additionally dispatch the maritime patrol aircraft P-8A, explosive ordnance disposal teams, and unmanned minesweeping equipment. This represents a compromise that responds to U.S. requests for military deployment while attempting to avoid direct military confrontation with Iran. This measure should serve as an opportunity to safeguard international maritime routes and demonstrate Korea's responsible role in the international community.\n\nThe Strait of Hormuz is a critical juncture for energy security, with over 70 percent of our crude oil imports passing through it. Given the current reality of maritime threats, including recent attacks on Korean vessels, sharing responsibility with the international community for freedom of navigation aligns with our national interests. The expanded operation will double the Cheonghae Unit's operational radius. In particular, the preemptive deployment of explosive ordnance disposal teams and unmanned minesweeping equipment establishes a foundation for participating in mine clearance and route restoration in case of emergency, representing a forward-looking strategic move.\n\nHowever, we must also acknowledge the practical limitations and dilemmas inherent in this measure. By excluding the interior of the Strait of Hormuz and the Gulf of Oman from the operational area to avoid confrontation with Iran, the operation has an inherent weakness: it cannot directly escort our vessels passing through the strait. The government faces a difficult challenge of securing vessel safety while avoiding conflict with Iran.\n\nThe government must manage operational guidelines thoroughly to avoid unnecessary friction with Iran while establishing independent countermeasures to ensure vessel safety. Additionally, given that the P-8A maritime patrol aircraft to be deployed represents core military capability for monitoring maritime threats including North Korean submarines, the government must carefully prepare alternative asset deployment plans to ensure uninterrupted domestic surveillance, reconnaissance, and anti-submarine operations.\n\nWe hope this measure can serve as a foundation for strengthening Korea-U.S. security cooperation, which has shown differences over intelligence sharing on North Korea and the transition of wartime operational control. We must expand common ground between allies based on shared understanding of maritime route protection. The government must transparently explain to the National Assembly the purpose of the expanded mission and measures to ensure troop safety, and obtain its consent. We urge the government to exercise meticulous follow-up measures to ensure that contributions to the international community do not undermine our own security and national interests.",
